Orchestrator + Judge Gate for Long Sequential Tasks

Overview
This spec builds a single orchestrator session in Claude Code that runs a long list of similar tasks (your 150) to completion, without you re-typing "continue." Three roles do the work:
1. Orchestrator — the main session. Holds only the checklist and dispatches one task at a time. It does no task work itself, so its context stays small.
2. Worker subagent — spawned once per task, in its own fresh context. Does the actual work, verifies it, reports one short line back.
3. Judge Stop hook — an agent-type hook that fires whenever the orchestrator tries to end its turn. It reads the original request from the session transcript, checks the work against stated acceptance criteria, and forces the turn to continue if anything is unmet.
The judge is the generic part: it grades "did you finish what was asked" against criteria the task itself declares, so the same hook works on any long task, not just checkbox lists. The acceptance-criteria file is what keeps a language-model judge honest.
Use this when a job has many sequential steps and you want it to run unattended or semi-attended. For a job you will babysit anyway, the checklist alone is enough and the judge is optional — see Failure Modes. For fully independent tasks at scale, an external driver script is more robust than any single session; this spec is the single-orchestrator design you asked for.
Architecture
Orchestrator (main session). You launch it once with a kickoff prompt (Component 2). Its whole job is to walk the task list in order and, for each item, spawn a worker and record the result. It keeps PROGRESS.md — one line per task — as the single source of truth for what is done. Because it never does task work in its own context, 150 workers' worth of detail never accumulates there; only 150 short result lines do.
Worker subagent (one per task). Defined in .claude/agents/task-worker.md (Component 1). Each spawn is a fresh context that receives exactly one task plus a pointer to the shared spec. It does the work, runs whatever per-task verification you define, and returns a one-line verdict (DONE <id> or FAILED <id>: <reason>). The tiny reply is deliberate: it is all that flows back into the orchestrator.
Judge Stop hook (fires on every turn end). Defined in .claude/settings.json (Component 3) as a type: "agent" hook on the Stop event. When the orchestrator tries to end its turn, the hook spawns a verifier subagent that reads the original request from the transcript, reads the acceptance criteria, checks the actual artifacts, and returns {"ok": true} to allow the stop or {"ok": false, "reason": ...} to force the turn to continue with reason as the next instruction.
Flow per task:
1. Orchestrator reads the next unchecked line in PROGRESS.md.
2. It spawns task-worker with that task.
3. Worker does the task, self-verifies, returns its one-line verdict.
4. Orchestrator marks the line [x] (done) or [!] (failed, with reason) and moves on.
5. When it thinks it is finished and tries to stop, the Stop hook fires.
6. The judge checks completion against the criteria. If unmet, it returns ok:false and the orchestrator keeps going; if met, the turn ends.
The two checks are layered on purpose: the worker's own verification catches a bad result at the moment it is produced (task 12, not task 150), and the judge is the final gate on "is the whole job actually done."
The acceptance-criteria contract
This one convention is what lets a language-model judge generalize without being gullible. The problem with an LLM judge that reads the orchestrator's own summary is that a confident "all 150 tasks completed" can talk it into approving. The fix: the judge never trusts a claim it cannot verify, and it checks against criteria written before the work starts, not against the narrative produced after.
The convention. At the very start of any long task, the orchestrator writes a DONE_CRITERIA.md file listing the concrete, checkable conditions for "done." The judge reads this file every time it fires. If the file is absent, the judge approves the stop immediately — that is what keeps the same hook harmless on short, everyday sessions.
What goes in it — checkable, not vague:
• Each criterion is something the judge can confirm by reading a file, running a command, or counting, not by reading prose. Good: "PROGRESS.md contains 150 lines and none begins with - [ ]." Bad: "all tasks are done."
• Point at artifacts: output files that must exist, a test or lint command that must exit 0, row counts, a summary file the workers append to.
• Keep it short. Five to ten lines covering the real finish conditions beats an exhaustive list the judge will skim.
Example DONE_CRITERIA.md for the 150-task job:
# Done criteria
- PROGRESS.md has exactly 150 task lines.
- No line in PROGRESS.md starts with "- [ ]" (all dispatched).
- Every line marked "- [!]" has a failure reason and appears in FAILURES.md.
- `make verify` exits 0.
- output/ contains one result file per completed task id.
The judge's job then reduces to the same mechanical loop on every task: read the criteria, check each one against reality, approve only if all pass. That is most of the determinism of a hard-coded grep check, while staying general enough to reuse.
File layout
project/
├── .claude/
│   ├── settings.json            # registers the judge Stop hook
│   └── agents/
│       └── task-worker.md       # the per-task worker subagent
├── TASK_SPEC.md                 # shared rules every worker follows
├── PROGRESS.md                  # 150 checklist lines; source of truth
├── DONE_CRITERIA.md             # what "done" means; the judge reads this
├── FAILURES.md                  # workers/orchestrator log failed tasks here
└── output/                      # per-task result files
• .claude/settings.json and .claude/agents/ are project-scoped, so they apply only in this project and can be committed. (Hooks in .claude/settings.json require you to trust the folder; see Permissions.)
• TASK_SPEC.md holds the instructions common to all 150 tasks so each worker gets the same spec without the orchestrator repeating it in every dispatch.
• PROGRESS.md is both the work queue and the record. One line per task, e.g. - [ ] 001 <task description>.
• DONE_CRITERIA.md is the contract from the previous section.
• output/ is wherever the real deliverables land; the criteria reference it.
Component 1 — worker subagent
Save as .claude/agents/task-worker.md. The frontmatter tools line restricts the worker to what a task needs; narrow it to the real set so a worker can't wander. The body is its whole instruction set.
---
name: task-worker
description: Performs exactly one task from the run. Invoked by the orchestrator, one spawn per task.
tools: Read, Edit, Write, Bash
---

You perform ONE task and then stop. You are given a single task id and its
description in the prompt.

Steps:
1. Read TASK_SPEC.md for the rules that apply to every task.
2. Do the one task you were given. Do not touch other tasks or other files.
3. Verify your own work using the check defined in TASK_SPEC.md (run the test /
   lint / assertion for this task). If it does not pass, fix and retry up to
   twice.
4. Write your result to output/<id>.<ext> as TASK_SPEC.md specifies.
5. Reply with ONE line and nothing else:
   - "DONE <id>" if the task and its verification passed.
   - "FAILED <id>: <one-line reason>" if it could not be completed. Also append
     that line to FAILURES.md.

Keep your reply to that single line. Do not summarize, do not explain, do not
ask questions.
Why these choices:
• One task per spawn keeps each worker's context clean and independent, which is the whole reason to use subagents instead of doing the work inline.
• Self-verification inside the worker means a failure is caught and either fixed or reported at the moment it happens.
• The one-line reply is what makes 150 results fit in the orchestrator's context. A verbose worker would defeat the design.
• Bounded retry (twice, then report FAILED) prevents one hard task from stalling the whole run; you clean up failures in a final pass.
Component 2 — orchestrator kickoff
Before the run, create DONE_CRITERIA.md, TASK_SPEC.md, and PROGRESS.md (150 lines of - [ ] <id> <desc>). Then start the session and give the orchestrator this prompt:
You are an orchestrator. You do NOT perform tasks yourself.

The work queue is PROGRESS.md, one task per line. For each line that still
starts with "- [ ]", in top-to-bottom order:
  1. Dispatch the task-worker subagent, passing that line's id and description.
  2. When it replies, update the line in PROGRESS.md:
     - "- [x] <id> ..." if the worker returned DONE.
     - "- [!] <id> ... (reason)" if it returned FAILED.
  3. Move to the next unchecked line.

Complete every task without pausing to ask me for confirmation. Do not stop or
summarize while any line still starts with "- [ ]". When none remain, give me a
short summary: how many succeeded, and the ids and reasons of any that failed.
Notes:
• "You do NOT perform tasks yourself" is the load-bearing line. Without it the orchestrator will sometimes start doing the work in its own context and fill up.
• The explicit "do not pause to ask" cuts down on the most common early-stop behavior even before the hook is involved.
• Dispatch one worker at a time as written here. If your 150 tasks are fully independent and touch separate files, you can tell the orchestrator to run a few workers in parallel — but only then, or they will collide.
• The orchestrator, not you, edits PROGRESS.md after each result. That file surviving compaction is what lets the run resume its place if context is summarized.
Component 3 — the judge Stop hook
This is the generic completion gate. It is a type: "agent" hook on the Stop event: an agent hook spawns a subagent with tool access (Read, Grep, Glob, and more), so it can verify against real artifacts rather than trust a summary. Register it in .claude/settings.json:
{
  "hooks": {
    "Stop": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "agent",
            "prompt": "A Claude Code session is trying to end its turn. Decide whether the task it was given is actually complete.\n\nSteps:\n1. Read the user's original request from the transcript at the path in the input JSON (transcript_path). That request is the source of truth for what was asked.\n2. If a DONE_CRITERIA.md file exists in the working directory, read it and treat each line as a required condition. If it does NOT exist, respond {\"ok\": true} and stop — you have no criteria to enforce.\n3. Verify each criterion yourself: read the named files, run the named commands, count what needs counting. Do NOT trust any claim of completion you cannot confirm firsthand.\n4. If every criterion passes, respond exactly {\"ok\": true}.\n5. If any criterion is unmet, respond {\"ok\": false, \"reason\": \"<what remains, as a direct instruction to continue>\"}.\n\nInput JSON: $ARGUMENTS",
            "timeout": 180
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
The response contract (this is different from command hooks). Prompt and agent hooks do NOT use decision: "block". They return:
• {"ok": true} → the stop is allowed, the turn ends.
• {"ok": false, "reason": "..."} → on a Stop hook, reason is fed back to Claude and it keeps working, using reason as its next instruction.
Agent-hook specifics (from the current docs): default timeout 60s (raise it with timeout, in seconds, as above), up to 50 tool-use turns, and it does not support the prompt-hook impossible field. $ARGUMENTS is replaced with the hook's input JSON, which includes transcript_path, cwd, session_id, and stop_hook_active.
Prompt hook as the lighter alternative. If your criteria are checkable from the transcript alone and you don't need to run commands, use type: "prompt" instead — a single fast-model call (Haiku by default; set model for more). Its contract adds two useful fields: {"ok": false, "reason": ..., "impossible": true} lets the model concede a condition can never be met so the turn is allowed to end, and continueOnBlock controls tool-event behavior (not needed for Stop). A prompt-hook version of the gate:
{
  "hooks": {
    "Stop": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "prompt",
            "prompt": "Read DONE_CRITERIA.md if present. If absent, respond {\"ok\": true}. Otherwise judge from the transcript whether every criterion is met. If yes, {\"ok\": true}. If not, {\"ok\": false, \"reason\": \"what remains\"}."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Recursion / loop guard. Claude Code overrides a Stop hook after it blocks 8 times in a row without progress, then ends the turn with a warning. Two things keep you inside that: the judge only blocks on genuinely unmet criteria (so real progress clears blocks), and it returns ok:true the moment criteria pass. If a run legitimately needs more than 8 blocks to converge, raise the cap with the CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP environment variable. A command-hook gate reads stop_hook_active from its input and exits early when true to avoid looping; the agent/prompt hook expresses the same intent by conceding (prompt hook's impossible) or simply passing once done.
Permissions and safety
Pre-allow the tools the run needs. A permission prompt at task 47 stalls an unattended run exactly like an early stop does — the session sits waiting for approval that never comes. Decide up front what the workers need and grant it, so nothing blocks mid-run. Options, least to most permissive:
• Set specific allow rules in .claude/settings.json (the permissions.allow list) for the exact tools/commands the workers use, e.g. Read, Edit, Write, Bash(make:*). This is the safe default.
• Run in acceptEdits mode if the work is mostly file edits.
• Use --dangerously-skip-permissions only in a disposable sandbox or container, never against anything you care about. It removes the guardrail entirely.
Hooks and trust. A Stop hook in .claude/settings.json runs once you've trusted the project folder (the workspace-trust dialog). Note the direction of hook power: a hook can tighten restrictions (a deny blocks even in bypass mode) but a hook returning allow does not loosen deny rules. The judge only ever forces more work, so it's on the safe side of this.
The block cap is a safety limit, not a bug. The 8-in-a-row Stop-block override exists so a misfiring gate can't trap a session forever. Treat hitting it as a signal that either the criteria can't be met or the workers aren't making progress — investigate rather than blindly raising CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP. Raise the cap only when you know the job genuinely needs more iterations to converge.
Scope the hook to this project. Because the gate lives in project settings, it fires only in this project's sessions. If you ever move it to user settings (~/.claude/settings.json), it will fire on every session including quick questions — the no-criteria-means-approve rule keeps that harmless, but project scope is cleaner.
Failure modes and how the design handles each
Failure
What it looks like
How the design handles it
Early stop
Orchestrator ends with "I've done 30, want me to continue?"
The Stop hook checks criteria and returns ok:false, forcing it to continue. The kickoff prompt's "don't pause to ask" reduces how often this happens in the first place.
Bad result
A task is marked done but the output is wrong
Caught at two layers: the worker's own verification at production time, and the judge's criteria (e.g. make verify exits 0) at the end.
Judge loop
Hook keeps blocking, no progress
Capped at 8 consecutive blocks, then the turn is allowed to end with a warning. Signals a real problem to investigate.
Compaction
Context fills, gets summarized mid-run
State lives in PROGRESS.md, not the orchestrator's memory. After compaction it re-reads the file and resumes at the first unchecked line.
Permission stall
Session waits forever on an approval prompt
Pre-allowed tools mean no mid-run prompt. This looks like an early stop but isn't the model's doing — it's the approval gate.
One hard task
A single item can't be completed
Worker retries twice, then returns FAILED and logs to FAILURES.md. The run continues; you do a cleanup pass at the end.
Do you even need the judge? For a job you're checking on periodically, no — PROGRESS.md already lets you type "continue" and resume exactly where it left off, losing nothing. The judge earns its place when you want the run to finish unattended, or when "done" is a judgment call the criteria file can capture. For a babysat run, the checklist plus the kickoff prompt's anti-pause instruction is enough.
Test plan
Validate the machinery on a tiny run before committing 150 tasks to it.
1. Dry-run the worker alone. Put 3 tasks in PROGRESS.md, run the orchestrator, and confirm each spawns a worker, the worker writes to output/, and the line flips to [x]. Check that a deliberately-impossible task ends up [!] with a reason in FAILURES.md rather than looping.
2. Test the judge fires. With 3 unchecked tasks, interrupt or prompt the orchestrator to stop early. Confirm the Stop hook blocks and it resumes. /hooks in the session shows the hook is registered; run claude --debug (or /debug mid-session) to see the hook's matches, its ok/reason output, and any errors.
3. Test the judge passes. Let the 3-task run finish. Confirm the hook returns ok:true and the turn actually ends — i.e. the gate isn't stuck rejecting a genuinely complete run.
4. Test the no-criteria path. In an unrelated quick session (or with DONE_CRITERIA.md renamed away), confirm the hook approves the stop immediately, so it doesn't interfere with normal work.
5. Check the cap behavior. Optionally, set criteria that can't be met and confirm the run stops after 8 blocks with the override warning rather than hanging.
Only once 1–4 pass, scale PROGRESS.md up to 150 and start the real run. Keep claude --debug-file /tmp/run.log writing so you have a trace if something stalls overnight.
