Decision logging: two streams
To audit the branches other agents took, the judge needs two things that a single log can't give it: what actually happened and why the agent thought it was right. And you can't trust an agent's after-the-fact account of its own reasoning any more than you can trust "all 150 done" — so capture both independently and let the judge cross-check them. A stated rationale that doesn't match the recorded actions is itself the signal that a branch went wrong.
Stream 1 — the action log (automatic, hook-written). A PostToolUse hook appends one line per edit/write/command, tagged with which agent did it. No agent cooperation is involved, so it can't be skipped or spun. This is ground truth. Because settings-file hooks also fire inside subagents, and the input carries agent_id/agent_type there, every worker's actions are captured and attributed:
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write|Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "jq -c '{ts:(now|todate), agent:(.agent_type//\"orchestrator\"), agent_id:(.agent_id//\"main\"), tool:.tool_name, input:.tool_input}' >> \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/actions.jsonl\""
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Stream 2 — the decision log (agent-authored, enforced). The action log shows what an agent did but not why it chose that branch over another. So each worker also writes a structured decision record at every non-trivial fork: the choice, the alternatives it rejected, and the reason. One file per task (decisions/<id>.jsonl) keeps concurrent workers from stepping on each other's appends. One line per decision:
{"task":"042","agent":"task-worker","phase":"approach","chose":"stream-parse","alternatives":["load-whole-file"],"why":"input can exceed memory; spec says stay under 512MB","evidence":"TASK_SPEC.md line 14"}
The judge reads both: the decision log tells it which branch was taken and the claimed justification; the action log lets it confirm the agent actually did that, and against TASK_SPEC.md it can judge whether the branch was legitimate.
Forcing the logging: three layers
"Tell the agent to log its decisions" fails for the same reason "finish all 150" fails — it's soft. Make it deterministic in layers, strongest first.
Layer 1 — the action log can't be skipped. It's the PostToolUse hook above. The agent doesn't opt in; Claude Code fires it on every matching tool call, in the orchestrator and inside every worker. This layer needs no cooperation and is your reliable backstop.
Layer 2 — enforce the decision log with a SubagentStop hook. The worker's contract (its agent file) requires a decision record before it finishes; a SubagentStop hook makes that requirement binding by refusing to let a worker stop until the record exists. SubagentStop matched on the worker's agent type fires when each worker finishes, and exit 2 keeps it from stopping. Add to .claude/settings.json:
{
  "hooks": {
    "SubagentStop": [
      {
        "matcher": "task-worker",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/require-decision-log.sh\""
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
The script reads the worker's final line from last_assistant_message in the hook input (the DONE <id> / FAILED <id> line), then checks that decisions/<id>.jsonl exists and is non-empty. If not, it exits 2 with a reason, which is fed back so the worker keeps working and logs:
#!/bin/bash
INPUT=$(cat)
ID=$(echo "$INPUT" | jq -r '.last_assistant_message' | grep -oE '(DONE|FAILED) [0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1)
if [ -z "$ID" ] || [ ! -s "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/decisions/$ID.jsonl" ]; then
  echo "No decision record for task $ID. Append your branch decisions to decisions/$ID.jsonl before finishing." >&2
  exit 2
fi
exit 0
Add the matching requirement to the worker's agent file: "Before you reply, append one line to decisions/<id>.jsonl for each non-trivial choice you made (approach, interpretation of an ambiguous task, error handling), with chose, alternatives, why, and evidence."
Layer 3 — the orchestrator logs its own branches. The orchestrator's auditable decision is the mapping from a worker's verdict to what it wrote in PROGRESS.md. Have it append an orchestrator.jsonl record per dispatch — task id, verdict received, action taken — so the judge can confirm it didn't mismark or skip anything. If the orchestrator uses the task list instead of a flat file, TaskCreated and TaskCompleted hooks can capture those transitions automatically, the same way PostToolUse captures tool calls.
Note for parallel workers. All three layers write append-only. Per-task decision files (decisions/<id>.jsonl) already avoid collisions; for the shared actions.jsonl, POSIX appends under a few KB are atomic, so concurrent workers are generally safe, but if you fan out widely, give each worker its own actions/<id>.jsonl and let the judge glob them.
How the judge audits branches (and catches taint)
With both logs in place, the judge stops grading only outputs and starts grading reasoning. Add these to DONE_CRITERIA.md so the judge checks them:
• Coverage — every task marked [x] has a decision record and an action-log entry.
• Consistency of stated vs actual — each record's why matches what the action log shows the agent actually did. A worker that claimed one branch but did another is flagged, not passed.
• Legitimacy — each chose is allowed by TASK_SPEC.md; no worker took a branch the spec forbids (wrote to a shared file, skipped the verification step, used a disallowed approach).
• Verdict integrity — the orchestrator's PROGRESS.md mark matches the worker's actual verdict and the output that exists.
Catching taint specifically — audit per task, not just at the end. The worry is that a wrong branch at task 12 silently poisons 13–150. End-of-run auditing finds it, but only after 138 tasks built on it. To stop propagation, put a second SubagentStop hook on the worker — an agent-type one — that audits each worker's decision the moment it finishes:
{
  "hooks": {
    "SubagentStop": [
      {
        "matcher": "task-worker",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "agent",
            "prompt": "A worker just finished. Read its decision records in decisions/ for the task id in its final message, and its actions in actions.jsonl. Verify: each choice is permitted by TASK_SPEC.md, the stated reason matches the actions taken, and no choice contradicts a convention already recorded in CONVENTIONS.md. Respond {\"ok\": true} if sound, or {\"ok\": false, \"reason\": \"which branch is wrong and what to do instead\"} to send the worker back. Input: $ARGUMENTS"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Both SubagentStop hooks fire together and combine (most restrictive wins), so the cheap command check enforces that a decision was logged and this agent check judges whether the branch was right — the second is what actually prevents a bad decision from tainting the rest. It costs one model call per worker, which is worth it precisely when later tasks depend on earlier ones.
Guarding against drift across tasks. Some branches set a precedent the whole run should follow (a naming scheme, a shared data format). Have the first worker that makes such a call record it in a CONVENTIONS.md; later workers read it before deciding, and both the per-task and end-of-run judges check the decision log for any record that contradicts it. A contradiction in the log is the fingerprint of exactly the tainting you're trying to catch — one agent quietly diverging from what the others assumed.
The layering, end to end: the worker's own verification catches a broken result at production time; the per-task agent judge catches an unsound branch before the next task builds on it; the end-of-run judge catches anything that slipped through and confirms the whole run's reasoning holds together. The logs are what make the last two possible — without them the judges are grading a summary, which is the one thing you can't trust.
Tuning log volume for tool-heavy workflows
If your workflows fire a lot of tools, the blanket PostToolUse action log from Decision logging: two streams is the wrong granularity — it records the whole movie when the judge only needs the net change. Drop it and capture what each task changed, not every call it made. The agent-authored decision log (a few lines per task) stays as-is; only the action stream gets lighter. The intermediate churn — exploratory reads, scratch commands — can't taint anything; only the net effect and the reasoning can.
Best, if you're in git: let git hold only vetted work. Commit each task's work with the id in the message — but run the judge before the commit, not after (see Judge before the commit below), so a wrong branch never lands in history. Then git log per task is your audit trail: a record of judged states, zero separate logging, with each commit doubling as the evidence for that task's decision record.
Not in git: snapshot the change once per task. A single SubagentStop command hook fires when each worker finishes and appends one compact line — the diffstat, or the files that landed:
{
  "hooks": {
    "SubagentStop": [
      {
        "matcher": "task-worker",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/log-changeset.sh\""
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
#!/bin/bash
ID=$(cat | jq -r '.last_assistant_message' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1)
echo "$ID  $(git diff --stat | tail -1)" >> "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/changes.log"
Outside a repo, swap the git diff --stat for a listing of what landed — e.g. files under output/ newer than a run-start marker. Either way it's ~1 line per task instead of thousands.
If you truly need per-call detail, filter to the consequential calls. Most tool calls in a heavy workflow are reads and routine commands that can't take a wrong branch. Use the handler if field to log only the mutations that represent a real decision — commits, deletes, network, writes to tracked paths — and let everything else go unlogged:
{
  "matcher": "Bash",
  "hooks": [
    { "type": "command", "if": "Bash(git commit *)", "command": "...log-action.sh" },
    { "type": "command", "if": "Bash(rm *)",         "command": "...log-action.sh" },
    { "type": "command", "if": "Bash(curl *)",       "command": "...log-action.sh" }
  ]
}
If the heaviness is parallel fan-out rather than sequential volume, PostToolBatch fires once per resolved batch instead of once per call, collapsing each fan-out into a single record.
The judge's cross-check adjusts to match. Instead of matching every call against the stated reason, it checks that the net change and final artifacts line up with the branch the decision log claims. That's a stronger check anyway — it grades the outcome, not the keystrokes — and it stays cheap across hundreds of tasks.
Judge before the commit
If git history should hold only vetted work, the audit has to run before the commit lands — reading commits after the fact is too late. So move the gate onto the commit itself. A PreToolUse hook matched on the commit command fires before it runs and can block it; make it an agent hook that reviews what's about to be committed against the decision log, and the commit only goes through if the branch is sound.
The per-task flow becomes: worker does the work → writes its decision records → attempts git commit. The gate fires on that commit, reads the diff about to land (git diff HEAD), the task's decisions/<id>.jsonl, and TASK_SPEC.md, and allows or denies. On denial the reason is returned to the worker as the commit's error, so it fixes the branch and re-commits. Nothing wrong ever enters history.
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "agent",
            "if": "Bash(git commit *)",
            "prompt": "A worker is about to commit a task's work. Read the diff about to be committed (git diff HEAD), the task's decision records under decisions/ for the id in that work, and TASK_SPEC.md. Verify every branch it took is permitted, its stated reason matches the diff, and nothing contradicts CONVENTIONS.md. Respond {\"ok\": true} to allow the commit, or {\"ok\": false, \"reason\": \"which branch is wrong and what to do instead\"} to block it. Input: $ARGUMENTS"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Two properties make this the right boundary. PreToolUse fires before the commit runs and before any permission check, so a deny stops the commit even in bypass mode — the gate can't be skipped. And on PreToolUse, an agent hook's {"ok": false} returns the reason to the worker and lets it keep working, which is exactly the fix-and-re-commit loop you want rather than a hard stop.
This also settles the logging question from the previous section: once only vetted commits can land, git log per task is the audit trail — a record of judged states, not raw activity. You don't need a separate post-hoc action log; the gate upstream of the commit is what gives the history its meaning. The agent-authored decision log still exists, but as the input the gate reasons over, written just before the commit it defends.
Parallel workers need isolation here. A shared working tree can't cleanly gate one task's commit while another worker is mid-edit. If you fan out, give each worker its own git worktree/branch (Claude Code can spawn a subagent with isolation: "worktree"), let each commit behind its own gate, and have the orchestrator merge the vetted branches. Sequential workers sidestep this entirely — the tree only ever holds one task's changes.
The end-of-run Stop judge stays as a final backstop, but it's now confirming an already-vetted history rather than being the first line of defense.
Open-ended goals: discovery + outcome gate
Everything above assumes an enumerable list — you know the 150 tasks and put them in PROGRESS.md upfront. A goal like "close this coverage gap, which takes about ten items" is a different shape: the item list doesn't exist yet, the count is an output of research, not an input, and "done" is the gap being closed, not a checklist being finished. Run the fixed-list machinery at it unchanged and it will faithfully execute whatever items the research happened to produce and then declare victory on the checklist — even if the research missed half the gap. Two additions fix that.
1. Make discovery an explicit, gated phase. Add a phase-zero worker whose job is to research the gap and emit a candidate item list, each item tagged with the specific sub-gap it closes and how "done" for that item will be verified. Then gate the plan before any execution starts: does the union of items actually span the gap (no obvious holes), are they non-overlapping, is each one concrete and checkable? This is the same pre-commit-gate idea, moved up to the planning level — because the decomposition is the root decision, and a wrong item list taints the entire run downstream. It is also the cheapest, highest-leverage place to keep yourself in the loop: eyeballing ~10 proposed items takes a minute and catches a doomed plan before any work is spent on it.
2. Define "done" as the measured gap, not the boxes checked. The Stop judge's DONE_CRITERIA.md must assert the outcome — run the coverage tool and confirm the metric hit target, or the specific cases that were uncovered now pass — not "all ten items complete." This is the whole game. If the items are done but the metric hasn't moved to target, the judge returns {"ok": false, "reason": "gap still open in region X; research additional items"}, which sends the run back for another discovery increment on the uncovered part. Progress resets the block cap, so a few increments are fine; a gap that genuinely can't be closed automatically hits the cap and stops with a warning, which is the correct outcome — it surfaces "I couldn't finish this" instead of pretending.
The orchestrator prompt gains one branch to support this: when the outcome gate reports the gap still open, dispatch the researcher again for the named region, execute the new items, and re-measure — loop until the metric is met or the cap trips.
The trap this structure exists to prevent: all ten items done, gap still open, run reports success. A judge grading the checklist passes it. A judge grading the measured gap catches it. That difference is exactly "ran the list to its end" versus "ran the problem to its solution."
When it runs clean vs. when to stay in the loop. If the gap is objectively measurable — a coverage percentage, a concrete set of cases that must pass — the outcome gate is a hard backstop and the whole thing can run mostly unattended. If "closed" is a judgment call with no clean metric, the outcome gate is weaker, so lean on the decomposition gate and keep a human checkpoint at the plan step; approving the item list is where your review buys the most.
